  "solution": {
    "approach": "Check if integer is a power of 2 using bitwise check (n > 0 and (n & (n - 1)) == 0).",
    "timeComplexity": "O(1)",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1)",
    "code": {
      "C": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    if (scanf(\"%lld\", &n) == 1) {\n        if (n > 0 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0) printf(\"YES\\n\");\n        else printf(\"NO\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n",
      "C++": "#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    if (cin >> n) {\n        if (n > 0 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0) cout << \"YES\\n\";\n        else cout << \"NO\\n\";\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n",
      "Java": "import java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        if (sc.hasNextLong()) {\n            long n = sc.nextLong();\n            System.out.println((n > 0 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0) ? \"YES\" : \"NO\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n",
      "Python3": "import sys\n\ndef main():\n    s = sys.stdin.read().strip()\n    if s:\n        n = int(s)\n        print(\"YES\" if n > 0 and (n & (n - 1)) == 0 else \"NO\")\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n",
      "JavaScript": "const fs = require('fs');\n\nfunction main() {\n    const s = fs.readFileSync(0, 'utf-8').trim();\n    if (s.length > 0) {\n        const n = parseInt(s, 10);\n        console.log(n > 0 && (n & (n - 1)) === 0 ? \"YES\" : \"NO\");\n    }\n}\n\nmain();\n"
    },
    "notes": "Ensure edge case and standard I/O format handling.",
    "editorial": "A positive integer is a power of two if and only if it has exactly one bit set in binary: (n > 0) && ((n & (n - 1)) == 0). Print 'YES' or 'NO'."
  },
  "hints": [
    "Powers of 2 have exactly one 1 bit in binary representation.",
    "The expression n & (n - 1) clears the lowest set bit.",
    "Print 'YES' or 'NO'."
  ],
